From 3552395d6ab7654a4ff546ce0450cd8d9a3d258f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: York Jasper Niebuhr <yjn@yjn-systems.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 23:02:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] C deferred constants

Applies to the 'releases/gcc-16.1.0' tag.

Some compile-time constants carry semantic information that is lost when
the C frontend folds them.  In particular, folding an offsetof expression
to an INTEGER_CST loses both the field it refers to and the fact that the
value depends on a field offset.

Preserving this information is required by transformations which finalize
or otherwise modify such values at a later compilation stage.  This is
needed for runtime structure layout randomization and can also be used by
eBPF CO-RE, where relocations depend on information about the source-level
object rather than only on its folded value.

Add deferred constants to allow such expressions to retain their semantic
origin while continuing to behave as compile-time constants where required.
Represent deferred expressions using DEFERRED_CST_EXPR and preserve
deferred field-offset dependencies while folding offsetof expressions.
Constant parts surrounding a deferred expression may be folded into the
deferred expression.

Allow deferred constants in static initializers when requested.  This
permits constructs such as addresses of structure members and offsetof
initializers to remain valid even though their final values are deferred.

Add an early IPA barrier for deferred constants.  This provides a point
before which an instrumentation pass can consume or transform the preserved
expressions.  Deferred constants which reach the normal barrier are
resolved to their compile-time values; a hard-barrier mode is provided to
diagnose any deferred constants which remain unexpectedly.

Signed-off-by: York Jasper Niebuhr <yjn@yjn-systems.com>
